<urn:atm:triage:finding/DAEMON-BOOTSTRAP-BOUNDARY-PLAN-1>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "DAEMON-BOOTSTRAP-BOUNDARY-PLAN-1" ;
  triage:title "DaemonBootstrapClient boundary drift leaves a stale atm-daemon-bootstrap dependent in the machine-readable contract" ;
  triage:description "The daemon-bootstrap boundary drift is between boundaries/atm-daemon-client/daemon-bootstrap.toml and docs/atm-daemon-client/boundaries.md, but the actual defect is in the TOML. The human-readable boundary and the accepted AC.8 plan both describe DaemonBootstrapClient as the thin-client bootstrap seam consumed by atm and atm-graft. The machine-readable record still allows atm-daemon-bootstrap as a dependent even though the current atm-daemon-bootstrap crate does not depend on atm-daemon-client and no longer owns endpoint/bin bootstrap helpers after AC.8. This is stale boundary widening, not a missing documentation update for a legitimate live edge." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-AD" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "ARCH" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
  triage:repeatable false ;
  triage:sweepScope "boundary record plus paired docs/plans" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-07-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/DAEMON-BOOTSTRAP-BOUNDARY-PLAN-1/integrate-AD/1> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/DAEMON-BOOTSTRAP-BOUNDARY-PLAN-1/integrate-AD/2> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/DAEMON-BOOTSTRAP-BOUNDARY-PLAN-1/integrate-AD/3> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/DAEMON-BOOTSTRAP-BOUNDARY-PLAN-1/integrate-AD/4> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/DAEMON-BOOTSTRAP-BOUNDARY-PLAN-1/integrate-AD/5> ;
  triage:openOn <urn:atm:triage:worktree/integrate-AD/develop-2026-07-10> ;
  triage:promoteTo <urn:atm:triage:worktree/integrate-AD/develop-2026-07-10> ;
  triage:notes """Root cause:

1. `boundaries/atm-daemon-client/daemon-bootstrap.toml:34` was widened in commit `9def47cb` on 2026-05-14 to `allowed_dependents = ["atm", "atm-daemon-bootstrap", "atm-graft"]`.
2. The paired prose boundary never adopted that third dependent. `docs/atm-daemon-client/boundaries.md:16-18,21-23,28-30` consistently describes the seam as the shared thin-client bootstrap path for `atm` and `atm-graft`.
3. The later AC.8 relock plan and closeout text also reaffirm that ownership: `docs/plans/phase-AC/sprint-AC8.md:97-110,123-135` and `docs/plans/phase-AC/plan-phase-AC.md:373-377` require `atm-daemon-client` to own the endpoint/bin bootstrap seam while `atm-graft` stops depending on `atm-daemon-bootstrap`.
4. The current code confirms there is no live `atm-daemon-bootstrap -> atm-daemon-client` edge to justify the widened dependent set. `crates/atm-daemon-bootstrap/Cargo.toml` depends only on `atm-core` and `atm-runtime`, and `crates/atm-daemon-bootstrap/src/lib.rs` only installs the retained runtime factory and forwards `with_default_roster_store()`.

Conclusion:
- this is not an unresolved prose-doc omission
- this is a stale machine-readable allowance that survived after the bootstrap seam moved out of `atm-daemon-bootstrap`

Remediation plan:

1. Tighten the machine-readable boundary:
   - change `boundaries/atm-daemon-client/daemon-bootstrap.toml` so `allowed_dependents = ["atm", "atm-graft"]`
   - keep `allowed_dependencies = ["atm-core", "atm-storage"]` and the existing forbidden edges unchanged unless a separate live-edge audit proves otherwise

2. Tighten the prose boundary to match the relocked ownership explicitly:
   - update `docs/atm-daemon-client/boundaries.md` to say the shared same-host bootstrap seam is consumed only by `atm` and `atm-graft`
   - add one explicit note that `atm-daemon-bootstrap` is not a consumer of this seam after AC.8; it remains a retained-runtime/roster transitional shim only

3. Tighten the planning artifacts so the same drift does not recur:
   - update `docs/plans/phase-AC/sprint-AC8.md` and `docs/plans/phase-AC/plan-phase-AC.md` only if they still imply `atm-daemon-bootstrap` is a legitimate dependent of DaemonBootstrapClient anywhere outside the historical migration narrative
   - otherwise leave the AC.8 narrative intact and cite it as the authority for the narrower dependent set

4. Add regression validation to the fix:
   - run `python3 .just/lint_boundaries.py`
   - run `cargo tree -p atm-daemon-client -e normal --prefix none`
   - run `! cargo tree -p atm-daemon-bootstrap -e normal --prefix none | rg "atm-daemon-client"`
   - run `rg -n "atm-daemon-bootstrap" boundaries/atm-daemon-client/daemon-bootstrap.toml docs/atm-daemon-client/boundaries.md`
   - closure requires the only remaining `atm-daemon-bootstrap` mentions to be historical/migration narrative, not current dependent-allowance text

5. Optional hardening if the boundary-lint framework already supports it cleanly:
   - add or extend a boundary-lint regression test so `BOUNDARY-DaemonBootstrapClient` fails if `allowed_dependents` grows beyond `atm` and `atm-graft` without an intentional same-PR update
   - do not invent a new complicated rule if the existing TOML + lint path already enforces the crate graph once the stale dependent is removed

Files directly impacted by the fix:
- `boundaries/atm-daemon-client/daemon-bootstrap.toml`
- `docs/atm-daemon-client/boundaries.md`
- possibly `docs/plans/phase-AC/sprint-AC8.md`
- possibly `docs/plans/phase-AC/plan-phase-AC.md`
- boundary-lint regression tests only if a simple existing test harness path already covers this boundary family

What not to change:
- do not widen the prose boundary to include `atm-daemon-bootstrap`; that would document a non-existent live dependent and would undo the AC.8 relock
- do not move bootstrap helper ownership back into `atm-daemon-bootstrap`
- do not treat `atm-daemon-bootstrap`'s transitional retained-runtime/roster shim as evidence that it owns the thin-client daemon bootstrap seam""" .

